<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Mexican Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1823">1823</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Mexican Empire was the monarchy established after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> won independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1821, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a> crowned as Emperor Agustín I in 1822. It briefly stretched from the northern frontier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, whose provinces were annexed in 1822. Opposition led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Antonio López de Santa Anna</a> forced Iturbide to abdicate in 1823, and Mexico became a federal republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-mexican-empire.jpg" alt="First Mexican Empire" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ludovicus Ferdinandus Can have elements by Sodacan and Heralder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandera_del_Primer_Imperio_Mexicano.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> broke apart after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1808, and in 1810 the priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-hidalgo-y-costilla">Miguel Hidalgo y Costilla</a> began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-war-of-independence">Mexican War of Independence</a>, a decade of fighting between insurgents and royalists. Hidalgo was executed in 1811 and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-morelos">José María Morelos</a> in 1815. When Liberals in Spain restored the Spanish Constitution of 1812 in 1820, curtailing the privileges of established elites, Mexican elites saw their interests threatened. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a>, an officer of the Spanish army, then drew up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-iguala">Plan of Iguala</a> and allied with the insurgent leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicente-guerrero">Vicente Guerrero</a>.</p><p>The plan rested on three guarantees: an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> governed by a Spanish prince, equal rights for all Americanos and Spaniards born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and the exclusive position of the Roman Catholic Church. Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-odonoju">Juan O'Donojú</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-cordoba">Treaty of Córdoba</a> on 24 August 1821. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-vii-of-spain">Ferdinand VII of Spain</a> rejected it and no Spanish prince would accept the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On the night of 18 May 1822 a sergeant, Pío Marcha, and the first infantry regiment began a demonstration for Iturbide to be made emperor, and crowds joined them. The next day Congress voted 67 to 15 in his favour, short of a legal quorum, and he was crowned on 21 July 1822. The treasury was bankrupt, the court was planned to outdo the viceroy's, and the government resorted to forced loans. Congress and the emperor clashed over a supreme court, a veto and military tribunals, and fifteen deputies were arrested in August on suspicion of a republican plot.</p><p>Iturbide dissolved Congress on 31 October 1822 and replaced it with a National Institutional Junta of his supporters. In December Antonio López de Santa Anna began a rebellion for a republic at Veracruz, and on 1 February 1823 officers proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-casa-mata">Plan of Casa Mata</a>, pledging to restore Congress. Most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> proclaimed for it by March. Iturbide reconvened Congress on 4 March, presented his abdication on 19 March, and left the capital after an agreement of 26 March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The empire's territory matched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">Viceroyalty of New Spain</a>, without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, and the Central American lands of the Captaincy General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> were annexed shortly after it began. It reached its maximum extent, from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, excluding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>.</p><p>After the abdication the Central American provinces formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a> on 1 July 1823, and only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a> stayed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Power passed to a republican provisional government in 1823 and 1824, which abolished the monarchy and established the First Mexican Republic. Later cessions, mostly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, reduced Mexico to less than half of its maximum extent.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Mexican Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Weak and divided, most of the provinces accepted annexation to Agustín de Iturbide’s <b>First Mexican Empire</b> in January 1822; San Salvador resisted until a Mexican army under Vicente Filísola occupied it in 1823.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 24 August 1821 the newly arrived Juan O’Donojú signed the Treaty of Córdoba, and in September Iturbide’s army entered Mexico City, founding the <b>First Mexican Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After independence from Spain in 1821 it resisted annexation to Agustín de Iturbide’s <b>Mexican Empire</b>, its congress even voting in 1822 to seek union with the United States.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After independence from Spain in 1821 and a brief annexation to Agustín de Iturbide’s <b>Mexican Empire</b>, Guatemala became the largest and most populous state of the Federal Republic of Central America, whose government sat in Guatemala City until 1834.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Nicaragua emerged from the break-up of the Federal Republic of Central America, the union formed by the former provinces of the Captaincy General of Guatemala after independence from Spain in 1821 and from Agustín de Iturbide’s <b>Mexican Empire</b> in 1823.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
